10 different conditions for class "0" (Does NOT read academic books most of the time):

- Reason for reading books is in [Entertainment Purpose, To increase my English vocabulary knowledge]
- Department is in [BBA, English, JMC, Others]
- Amount of time spent reading books per day < 1
- Frequency of reading books is in [A few times a month, Rarely, Never]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Fiction, Politics, Others]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [nan, 1-5]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [nan, 1-5]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is not "Increase Academic Performance"
- Who influenced you to read books is in [Friends, Self, Others]
- Do you read Newspaper is Yes

10 different conditions for class "1" (Reads academic books most of the time):

- Reason for reading books is Academic Purpose
- Department is in [CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, MCT, CIS, Textile]
- Amount of time spent reading books per day >= 1
- Frequency of reading books is in [Daily, A few times a week]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is Increase Academic Performance
- Who influenced you to read books is in [Teachers, Parents]
- How to manage books for reading is in [E-books, Borrow, Buy]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Non-Fiction, Biography]

---

These conditions capture tendencies and patterns that distinguish students who read academic books most of the time from those who do not.

---DIVIDER---

Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Gender: Gender likely has little or no direct causal effect on whether a student reads academic books most of the time.
- Age: Older students might be more serious about academic reading, but age differences among undergraduates are usually small.
- Department: Students in departments like CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, CIS, etc., are more likely to read academic books most of the time, while students in Arts or Business-related fields (e.g., English, BBA, JMC) might read less academic books or more leisure books.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More time spent reading books per day is strongly positively correlated with reading academic books most of the time.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," it strongly indicates reading academic books most of the time; other reasons like entertainment or vocabulary building are less likely to mean academic reading.
- How to manage books for reading: The method of managing books (E-books, Borrow, Buy, etc.) might have some weak association but is less directly causal.
- Number of e-books read in last two years: Higher numbers indicate more reading activity, possibly academic.
- Number of printed books read in last two years: Similarly, higher numbers indicate more reading activity.
- Favorite books for leisure time: Preference for Non-Fiction or Politics might indicate academic interest, whereas Fiction or Biography might indicate less academic reading.
- What is the change after reading books: "Increase Academic Performance" suggests academic reading.
- Who influenced you to read books: Self-Decision might indicate intrinsic motivation, possibly academic.
- Which language books do you like to read: Language preference is less directly related.
- Where do you read printed books: Location is less relevant.
- Do you read Newspaper: Probably unrelated.
- Format used for reading books: Printed or E-books is neutral.
- Frequency of reading books: Higher frequency (daily or weekly) indicates more academic reading; low frequency (a few times a month) suggests less academic reading.

